\ A closed db or stream handle stays closed. The registry slot or the file
\ descriptor it names is reissued to the next open, so without identity the old
\ handle would silently address the new resource; each handle carries a
\ generation and a stale one reports closed instead.

\ === database: a reissued slot does not revive a closed handle ===
":memory:" db-open to original
original "CREATE TABLE t (x)" [ ] db-exec drop
original "INSERT INTO t VALUES ('from-first')" [ ] db-exec drop
original "SELECT x FROM t" [ ] db-query :rows @ 0 @i :x @ . cr   \ from-first

original db-close
[: original "SELECT x FROM t" [ ] db-query :] catch drop :message @ . cr   \ database is closed

\ the next open takes the freed slot; the old handle must not reach it
":memory:" db-open to replacement
replacement "CREATE TABLE t (x)" [ ] db-exec drop
replacement "INSERT INTO t VALUES ('from-second')" [ ] db-exec drop
[: original "SELECT x FROM t" [ ] db-query :] catch drop :message @ . cr   \ database is closed
replacement "SELECT x FROM t" [ ] db-query :rows @ 0 @i :x @ . cr            \ from-second

original db-close                                                          \ idempotent on a stale handle
replacement db-close

clear
\ === stream: a reissued descriptor does not revive a closed handle ===
[ "printf" "AAA" ] start-process to producer
producer :out @ to output
output read . cr                                     \ AAA
output close
producer :in @ close  producer :err @ close  producer :pid @ wait drop

\ this start-process typically receives the same descriptor number back
[ "printf" "BBB" ] start-process to successor
[: output read :] catch drop :message @ . cr         \ stream is closed
successor :out @ read . cr                           \ BBB

output close                                         \ idempotent on a stale handle
successor :in @ close  successor :err @ close  successor :out @ close
successor :pid @ wait drop
